/* Agent Investigator course data, part 1: helpers + Beginner chapters 1-3 (fully authored) */
(() => {
  const Q = (q, ok, wr, why) => [q, ok, wr, why];
  const ch1 = { id:'b1', title:'Types of AI', lessons:[
   { t:'What Is AI?', v:[['Artificial intelligence','Computer systems that do tasks which normally need human thinking, such as learning or understanding language'],['Algorithm','A step-by-step set of instructions a computer follows'],['Training data','The examples an AI learns patterns from'],['Model','The finished program an AI produces after learning from data']],
     a:['Artificial intelligence, or AI, is the name for computer systems that can do tasks that usually need human thinking. Recognizing a face, translating a sentence and recommending a video are all examples.','Every AI runs on an algorithm, which is a set of step-by-step instructions. An AI learns from training data. After learning, it becomes a model, the finished program you actually use.'],
     b:['AI does not think the way you do. It finds patterns in huge amounts of data and uses them to make predictions. When a chatbot writes a sentence, it is predicting which words are most likely to come next.','This is why AI can be impressive and still be wrong. It has no understanding of truth. It has patterns, and patterns can mislead. That is why you stay in charge of every AI task.'], key:'AI finds patterns in data. It predicts. It does not know things the way people do.',
     q:[Q('What does an AI model learn from?','Training data',['Its own feelings','Rules written for each question'],'AI learns patterns from training data.'),Q('When a chatbot writes a sentence, what is it mostly doing?','Predicting likely next words',['Looking up the one true answer','Thinking about how it feels'],'Language models predict likely text based on patterns.'),Q('Which is the best description of an algorithm?','A step-by-step set of instructions',['A type of robot','A kind of internet connection'],'Algorithms are instructions a computer follows.')],
     g:{type:'sort',title:'AI or not AI?',intro:'Sort each item into the right group.',prompt:'Is each item an example of AI?',buckets:['Uses AI','Does not use AI'],items:[['A map app that predicts traffic jams',0],['A calculator adding 2 + 2',1],['A phone unlocking when it sees your face',0],['A light switch turning on a lamp',1],['A video app suggesting what to watch next',0],['A paper dictionary',1]],why:'AI learns from data to make predictions. A calculator or switch only follows fixed rules.'}},
   { t:'AI by Technology', v:[['Machine learning','AI that improves by finding patterns in data instead of following fixed rules'],['Natural language processing','AI that works with human language, such as chatbots and translators'],['Computer vision','AI that understands images and video'],['Robotics','Machines that sense the world and move or act in it']],
     a:['One way to sort AI is by the technology it uses. Machine learning is the biggest group. It learns from examples instead of being told every rule.','Natural language processing, or NLP, handles words. Computer vision handles pictures. Robotics combines sensors and motors so machines can act in the real world.'],
     b:['These technologies often work together. A delivery robot uses computer vision to see, machine learning to decide, and robotics to move. A voice assistant uses speech recognition and NLP.','When you meet a new AI tool, ask which technology sits underneath. That tells you what it is good at and where it is likely to fail.'], key:'Most modern AI is built on machine learning, then specialized for language, images or action.',
     q:[Q('Which technology lets AI understand pictures?','Computer vision',['Natural language processing','Robotics'],'Computer vision is AI for images and video.'),Q('A translator app mostly uses which technology?','Natural language processing',['Computer vision','Robotics'],'NLP works with human language.'),Q('A self-driving car needs which combination?','Vision, machine learning and robotics',['Only a calculator','Only a spell checker'],'Real tasks combine several technologies.')],
     g:{type:'match',title:'Match the technology',intro:'Match each technology with what it does.',pairs:[['Machine learning','Learns patterns from data'],['Computer vision','Understands photos and video'],['Natural language processing','Works with human language'],['Robotics','Senses and acts in the physical world']]}},
   { t:'AI by Capability', v:[['Narrow AI','AI built to do one kind of task well'],['General AI','Hypothetical AI that could learn any task a human can'],['Superintelligence','Hypothetical AI far smarter than any human'],['Hypothetical','Imagined or proposed but not real yet']],
     a:['Another way to classify AI is by capability, meaning how much it can do. All AI that exists today is narrow AI. It does one kind of task well, such as chess, translation or spotting spam.','General AI would learn any task a human can. Superintelligence would be far smarter than any person. Both are hypothetical, which means they are ideas, not real products.'],
     b:['Even tools that feel very smart, like modern chatbots, are still considered narrow. They are strong at language tasks but they do not match human flexibility, common sense or real-world experience.','Be careful when headlines say AI is about to become general or superintelligent. Real experts disagree about if and when that could happen. Judge tools by what they can do today.'], key:'Every AI you can use right now is narrow AI.',
     q:[Q('Which category describes all AI that exists today?','Narrow AI',['General AI','Superintelligence'],'Today\'s AI is narrow, even when it feels impressive.'),Q('What does hypothetical mean?','Imagined but not real yet',['Proven by experts','Extremely fast'],'General AI and superintelligence are hypothetical.'),Q('A chess program that cannot do anything else is…','Narrow AI',['General AI','Superintelligence'],'It does one task well.')],
     g:{type:'sort',title:'Classify the AI',intro:'Is it real narrow AI today, or hypothetical?',prompt:'Sort each description.',buckets:['Exists today (narrow)','Hypothetical'],items:[['A spam filter for email',0],['A machine that can learn any human job by itself',1],['A voice assistant that sets timers',0],['An AI far smarter than every human combined',1],['A tool that translates Spanish to English',0]],why:'Narrow AI is real. General AI and superintelligence are still ideas.'}},
   { t:'AI by Functionality', v:[['Reactive machine','AI that only reacts to the current situation and has no memory'],['Limited memory','AI that uses recent past data to decide'],['Theory of mind','Hypothetical AI that understands human emotions and beliefs'],['Self-aware AI','Hypothetical AI with its own consciousness']],
     a:['Functionality describes how an AI works with information. A reactive machine only responds to what is in front of it. An old chess computer is one.','Limited memory AI uses recent data. A self-driving car watches nearby cars for a few seconds to decide what to do. Theory of mind and self-aware AI are hypothetical.'],
     b:['Most AI you use today is limited memory. Chatbots keep some of your recent conversation in mind, but they are not remembering you the way a friend does.','Keep this in mind when you share things with AI. Some tools save your conversations and use them to improve. Always check the privacy settings before typing anything personal.'], key:'Today\'s tools are reactive or limited memory. Self-aware AI does not exist.',
     q:[Q('A self-driving car using the last few seconds of data is…','Limited memory AI',['Self-aware AI','Reactive machine'],'It uses recent data to decide.'),Q('Which type has no memory of the past?','Reactive machine',['Limited memory','Theory of mind'],'Reactive machines only react.'),Q('Which type is still hypothetical?','Self-aware AI',['Reactive machine','Limited memory'],'No AI is conscious.')],
     g:{type:'match',title:'Match the type',intro:'Match each functionality type to its description.',pairs:[['Reactive machine','Responds only to the present moment'],['Limited memory','Uses recent data to decide'],['Theory of mind','Would understand human emotions'],['Self-aware AI','Would have its own consciousness']]}},
   { t:'AI in Business', v:[['Automation','Using technology to do repeat tasks without a person'],['Chatbot','A program that talks with people by text or voice'],['Recommendation engine','AI that suggests products or content based on your behavior'],['Predictive analytics','Using data to forecast what will probably happen']],
     a:['Businesses use AI to save time and money. Automation handles repeat tasks. Chatbots answer customer questions at any hour.','Recommendation engines suggest what to watch or buy. Predictive analytics forecasts things like how many items a store will sell next week.'],
     b:['AI shows up in almost every industry. Hospitals use it to help read scans. Banks use it to spot fraud. Farms use it to check crop health. Schools use it for tutoring tools.','AI helps people, but it does not replace responsibility. A bank worker still reviews a flagged transaction. Understanding where AI fits at work is a skill employers value.'], key:'In business, AI handles repeat work and finds patterns. People handle judgment and responsibility.',
     q:[Q('Which AI suggests videos based on what you watched?','Recommendation engine',['Predictive spreadsheet','Spell checker'],'It learns from your behavior.'),Q('Using AI to forecast next week\'s sales is…','Predictive analytics',['Automation only','A chatbot'],'Forecasting from data is predictive analytics.'),Q('A bank flags a strange payment using AI. Who should make the final decision?','A trained person reviews it',['Nobody, the AI is always right','The customer\'s friend'],'People stay responsible for important decisions.')],
     g:{type:'scenario',title:'Run the help desk',intro:'You manage a small shop and want to add AI. Make good decisions.',steps:[{q:'Customers ask the same five questions all day. What do you do?',opts:[['Add a chatbot for those five, with a way to reach a person',3,'Good fit. Automation handles repeat questions and humans handle the rest.'],['Replace every employee with a chatbot',0,'Risky. Customers will get stuck when the bot cannot help.'],['Ignore the problem',1,'Safe but wastes time.']]},{q:'The chatbot gives a customer wrong store hours. What now?',opts:[['Fix its information and check answers regularly',3,'AI needs monitoring and corrections.'],['Blame the customer',0,'Never blame users for AI mistakes.'],['Turn it off forever without checking why',1,'Better to fix and improve.']]},{q:'Should the shop tell customers they are talking to an AI?',opts:[['Yes, be clear it is an AI',3,'Transparency builds trust.'],['No, hide it',0,'Hiding it can mislead people.'],['Only if they ask twice',1,'Better to be upfront.']]}]}},
   { t:'AI Tools: The Big Four', v:[['Generative AI','AI that creates new text, images, audio or code'],['Chatbot','A program you converse with'],['Prompt','The instruction or question you give an AI'],['Writing assistant','A tool that checks and improves your writing']],
     a:['Generative AI makes new content. Four tools students hear about most are ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude and Grammarly.','ChatGPT is a chatbot from OpenAI. Gemini is Google\'s AI assistant. Claude is an AI assistant from Anthropic. Grammarly is a writing assistant that checks grammar, clarity and tone.'],
     b:['Chatbots like ChatGPT, Gemini and Claude respond to prompts for explaining, brainstorming, summarizing and drafting. They are general helpers. Grammarly focuses on your own writing and suggests edits.','No tool is perfect. Each can make mistakes, and features and rules change often. Check what your school allows before you use any of them for schoolwork.'], key:'Pick the tool that fits the task, and follow your school\'s rules.',
     q:[Q('Which tool focuses on improving your own writing?','Grammarly',['ChatGPT','Gemini'],'Grammarly is a writing assistant.'),Q('Who makes Claude?','Anthropic',['OpenAI','Google'],'Claude is made by Anthropic.'),Q('What is a prompt?','The instruction you give an AI',['The AI\'s answer','A type of virus'],'You write the prompt.')],
     g:{type:'match',title:'Match the tool',intro:'Match each tool to its description.',pairs:[['ChatGPT','Chatbot from OpenAI'],['Gemini','AI assistant from Google'],['Claude','AI assistant from Anthropic'],['Grammarly','Writing assistant for grammar and tone']]}},
   { t:'Using Each Tool Well', v:[['Context','Background details that help an AI understand your request'],['Role prompt','Telling the AI who to act as'],['Iterate','Improve a result by asking again with changes'],['Review','Check AI output for mistakes before using it']],
     a:['Chatbots work best when you give context. Instead of "help with my essay", say what the essay is about, your grade level and what kind of help you want.','You can use a role prompt, such as "Act as a patient science tutor". You can also iterate, which means asking again with changes until the result is useful.'],
     b:['A good routine is ask, review, improve. Always review what the AI says. Check facts, look for strange claims, and make sure the work still sounds like you.','Grammarly works differently. It suggests edits inside your own writing. You decide which suggestions to accept. Accepting everything without reading can change your meaning.'], key:'Give context, review the result, then improve it. You make the final call.',
     q:[Q('Which prompt gives the best context?','I am in 9th grade writing about volcanoes. Explain how they form in simple words.',['Volcanoes','Do my homework'],'Specific context gets a more useful answer.'),Q('What does it mean to iterate?','Improve the result by asking again with changes',['Delete the chat','Accept the first answer'],'Iterating means refining.'),Q('What should you do before using AI output?','Review it for mistakes',['Copy it exactly','Assume it is correct'],'Always review.')],
     g:{type:'order',title:'The smart AI routine',intro:'Put the steps in the best order.',prompt:'Click the steps in order.',items:['Write a prompt with context','Read the answer carefully','Check any facts against a trusted source','Ask a follow-up to improve it','Finish the work in your own words']}},
   { t:'Choose Your Tool: Deep Dive', v:[['Strength','What a tool does especially well'],['Limitation','Something a tool cannot do or does poorly'],['Use case','A specific job you use a tool for'],['Compare','Look at two things to see how they differ']],
     a:['Every tool has strengths and limitations. A strength is what it does best. A limitation is where it struggles or fails.','Today you choose one tool to study closely. Think about its use case, the job it fits best, and how it compares with the others.'],
     b:['Chatbots can draft, explain and brainstorm, but they can state wrong facts confidently. A writing assistant is strong at grammar but may not know your teacher\'s expectations.','Knowing limitations protects you. If you know a tool tends to make things up, you will check its facts. That habit is the core skill of a responsible AI user.'], key:'Knowing a tool\'s limits is as important as knowing its strengths.',
     q:[Q('What is a limitation?','Something a tool cannot do or does poorly',['A tool\'s best feature','The price of a tool'],'Limitations are weaknesses.'),Q('What does comparing tools help you do?','Choose the best one for a job',['Avoid learning','Pick randomly'],'Comparing reveals strengths and limits.'),Q('Why should you know a tool\'s limits?','So you know when to double-check it',['So you can blame it','It does not matter'],'Limits tell you what to verify.')],
     g:{type:'pick',title:'Pick your tool and prove you know it',intro:'Choose one tool. Answer three questions about it.',prompt:'Which tool do you want to investigate?',options:[
       {name:'ChatGPT',tag:'Chatbot from OpenAI',qs:[Q('ChatGPT is made by…','OpenAI',['Google','Anthropic'],'ChatGPT comes from OpenAI.'),Q('What is a smart way to use ChatGPT for studying?','Ask it to explain a topic, then verify the facts',['Copy its answers into a test','Share your passwords with it'],'Use it to learn, then verify.'),Q('A known limitation of chatbots is…','They can state wrong things confidently',['They never make mistakes','They can read your mind'],'Always fact-check.')]},
       {name:'Gemini',tag:'AI assistant from Google',qs:[Q('Gemini is made by…','Google',['OpenAI','Grammarly'],'Gemini is Google\'s AI.'),Q('Which is a good Gemini task?','Summarizing a long article you provide, then checking details',['Taking a test for you','Sharing private info'],'Summaries help but must be checked.'),Q('What should you do with surprising claims?','Verify them with trusted sources',['Trust them','Post them immediately'],'Verify before sharing.')]},
       {name:'Claude',tag:'AI assistant from Anthropic',qs:[Q('Claude is made by…','Anthropic',['OpenAI','Google'],'Claude comes from Anthropic.'),Q('Which is a good use of Claude for school?','Ask it to explain your mistakes and then fix them yourself',['Have it write the whole assignment','Give it your home address'],'Use AI to learn, not to replace your work.'),Q('Even a careful assistant can…','Still make mistakes',['Never be wrong','Replace a teacher entirely'],'No AI is perfect.')]},
       {name:'Grammarly',tag:'Writing assistant',qs:[Q('Grammarly mainly helps with…','Grammar, clarity and tone in your writing',['Building robots','Making videos'],'It is a writing assistant.'),Q('What should you do with its suggestions?','Read each one and choose',['Accept all blindly','Ignore them all'],'You decide.'),Q('Using Grammarly well means your final work is…','Still your own ideas and voice',['Written by the tool','Always perfect'],'It polishes; it should not replace your thinking.')]}]}},
   { t:'AI Companies', v:[['Company','A business that builds and sells products'],['Open source','Software whose code is shared publicly so others can use it'],['Chip','A tiny computer part; AI needs powerful ones'],['Research lab','A team that studies and builds new technology']],
     a:['Many companies build AI. OpenAI makes ChatGPT. Google makes Gemini. Anthropic makes Claude. Microsoft builds AI into its products and partners with OpenAI. Meta shares some of its AI models openly.','Nvidia is known for chips, the powerful computer parts that train and run AI models.'],
     b:['Companies compete to build better AI, but they also face the same questions about safety, fairness and privacy. Reading how a company handles your data helps you decide whether to trust it.','Open source models can be used by anyone, which helps research and small businesses. It also means less control over how they are used. Both approaches have tradeoffs.'], key:'Know who makes a tool and read how they handle your data.',
     q:[Q('Which company is known for AI chips?','Nvidia',['Grammarly','Anthropic'],'Nvidia makes the chips used to train AI.'),Q('What does open source mean?','Code is shared publicly',['It costs money','It cannot be changed'],'Anyone can use open source code.'),Q('Why read a company\'s privacy policy?','To learn how your data is used',['To find jokes','It does not matter'],'Privacy policies explain data use.')],
     g:{type:'pick',title:'Company deep dive',intro:'Pick a company and prove what you learned.',prompt:'Which company do you want to explore?',options:[
       {name:'OpenAI',tag:'Maker of ChatGPT',qs:[Q('OpenAI makes…','ChatGPT',['Gemini','Claude'],'ChatGPT is OpenAI\'s chatbot.'),Q('Companies like OpenAI should be clear about…','How they use your data',['Nothing','Only their prices'],'Transparency matters.'),Q('Before using a tool from any company you should…','Check its rules and settings',['Ignore them','Give it all your info'],'Check settings first.')]},
       {name:'Google',tag:'Maker of Gemini',qs:[Q('Google\'s AI assistant is…','Gemini',['Claude','ChatGPT'],'Gemini is from Google.'),Q('If an AI summary seems wrong you should…','Check the original source',['Trust it','Share it fast'],'Verify.'),Q('AI tools in a school account may follow…','School rules and settings',['No rules','Only your guess'],'Schools set policies.')]},
       {name:'Anthropic',tag:'Maker of Claude',qs:[Q('Anthropic makes…','Claude',['Gemini','Grammarly'],'Claude is Anthropic\'s assistant.'),Q('A safe habit with any chatbot is…','Never share passwords or private details',['Share everything','Use someone else\'s account'],'Protect your privacy.'),Q('AI companies are…','Businesses that make products',['Always neutral','Always correct'],'They have goals and make mistakes.')]},
       {name:'Nvidia',tag:'AI chip maker',qs:[Q('Nvidia is best known for…','Chips that power AI',['A chatbot named Claude','A grammar checker'],'Its chips train and run models.'),Q('Why does AI need powerful chips?','Training uses huge amounts of computing',['It needs pretty screens','Chips write the answers by hand'],'AI math is heavy.'),Q('Compared with software makers, chip makers…','Build the hardware AI runs on',['Write all prompts','Replace teachers'],'Hardware matters too.')]}]}},
   { t:'Which Tool for Which Job?', v:[['Task match','Choosing the tool that best fits the job'],['Privacy setting','A control that decides what data a tool keeps'],['Free tier','The no-cost version of a tool, often with limits'],['Terms of service','The rules you agree to when you use a tool']],
     a:['Task match means choosing the right tool for the job. A chatbot is great for explaining and brainstorming. A writing assistant is better for polishing your own writing.','Before you start, look at privacy settings, the free tier limits and the terms of service. Many tools have age rules, so ask a teacher or parent if you are unsure.'],
     b:['Using the wrong tool wastes time and can cause problems. Using a chatbot to guess a fact you could check in a trusted source is a poor match.','Make a habit of asking three questions before you open any AI tool: What is my goal? Is this tool allowed here? What information am I about to share?'], key:'Goal, permission, privacy: ask these three every time.',
     q:[Q('Which job fits a writing assistant best?','Fixing grammar in your own paragraph',['Researching a fact from scratch','Building a robot'],'Writing assistants polish your writing.'),Q('What is the terms of service?','The rules you agree to when using a tool',['A tool\'s price','A secret code'],'It lists what you may do.'),Q('Before using any tool you should ask…','Is it allowed and what will I share?',['Is it popular?','Is it fast?'],'Permission and privacy first.')],
     g:{type:'sort',title:'Pick the best tool',intro:'Choose the best fit for each job.',prompt:'Which tool type fits each job?',buckets:['Chatbot','Writing assistant'],items:[['Explain how photosynthesis works in simple words',0],['Fix comma mistakes in my finished paragraph',1],['Brainstorm ideas for a science fair',0],['Make my email sound more polite',1],['Quiz me on vocabulary words',0]],why:'Chatbots help you explore ideas. Writing assistants polish text you wrote.'}},
   { t:'Chapter 1 Field Review', v:[['Narrow AI','AI built for one kind of task'],['Machine learning','AI that learns patterns from data'],['Generative AI','AI that creates new content'],['Prompt','The instruction you give an AI']],
     a:['You have covered a lot: what AI is, how to classify it by technology, capability and function, how businesses use it, the major tools and the companies behind them.','Before the final test, review the key terms above. They appear again and again in the rest of the course.'],
     b:['Remember the big ideas. All current AI is narrow. Most uses machine learning. Generative AI makes new content. Tools differ in strengths and limits. You choose the tool, review the output and stay responsible.','In the next chapter you will learn how to use AI responsibly, including rights, laws, privacy and fact-checking.'], key:'Know the types, choose the tool, review the result.',
     q:[Q('Which is true of all AI that exists today?','It is narrow AI',['It is self-aware','It is always correct'],'Today\'s AI is narrow.'),Q('Generative AI is AI that…','Creates new content',['Only sorts data','Only moves robots'],'It makes text, images or audio.'),Q('Who is responsible for checking AI output?','You',['The AI','Nobody'],'You stay in charge.')],
     g:{type:'spot',title:'Spot the false claims',intro:'A student wrote notes about AI. Some lines are false.',prompt:'Tap every line that is false.',lines:[['All AI today is narrow AI.',false],['Self-aware AI already exists in modern chatbots.',true,'No AI is conscious. Self-aware AI is hypothetical.'],['A prompt is the instruction you give an AI.',false],['AI is always right, so there is no need to check its answers.',true,'AI makes mistakes, so you check important facts.'],['Machine learning learns patterns from data.',false]]}}
  ]};

  const ch2 = { id:'b2', title:'Using AI Responsibly', lessons:[
   { t:'AI and Human Rights', v:[['Human rights','Basic freedoms and protections every person deserves'],['Fairness','Treating people equally and without bias'],['Accountability','Being responsible for the results of your choices'],['Consent','Clear permission given freely']],
     a:['Human rights are basic freedoms and protections every person deserves, like privacy, equal treatment and freedom of expression. AI affects these rights because it can make decisions about people.','Responsible AI use means fairness, accountability and consent. Fairness means treating people equally. Accountability means someone is responsible. Consent means people agree to how their information or image is used.'],
     b:['If an AI hiring tool favors one group, that is an unfairness problem. If a school uses face scanning without asking, that is a consent problem. If something goes wrong and nobody takes responsibility, that is an accountability problem.','You are a user and a future decision maker. Knowing these ideas lets you spot problems early and speak up.'], key:'Fairness, accountability, consent: three questions for any AI use.',
     q:[Q('What does consent mean?','Clear permission given freely',['Staying quiet','A strong password'],'Consent requires a real choice.'),Q('An AI hiring tool favors one group. Which value is broken?','Fairness',['Speed','Cost'],'Biased results are unfair.'),Q('Who is accountable for an AI-assisted decision?','A person or organization',['The AI alone','Nobody'],'Humans stay accountable.')],
     g:{type:'sort',title:'Rights check',intro:'Which right or value is involved?',prompt:'Match each situation to the main value at stake.',buckets:['Fairness','Consent','Accountability'],items:[['An AI grading tool gives lower scores to a group of students for no clear reason',0],['A classmate\'s photo is used in an AI image tool without asking them',1],['An app makes a harmful mistake and the company says it is not their problem',2],['A scholarship tool rejects people from certain neighborhoods more often',0],['A face is scanned at an event and nobody said they could opt out',1]],why:'Treating people unequally is fairness. Using people without permission is consent. Refusing to take responsibility is accountability.'}},
   { t:'US Laws and AI Protection', v:[['Privacy law','A rule that limits how personal information is collected and used'],['Impersonation','Pretending to be another person'],['Deepfake','AI-made fake image, audio or video of a real person'],['Regulation','Rules made by a government to guide behavior']],
     a:['Governments are still writing AI rules. In the United States there is no single law covering all AI, but many laws apply. Privacy laws limit how information is collected and used. Laws against fraud and impersonation also apply when AI is involved.','A deepfake is an AI-made fake image, audio or video of a real person. Creating deepfakes to trick, harass or embarrass people can break laws and school rules. Federal and state governments keep adding laws, including laws that target harmful fake images.'],
     b:['Some protections for students already exist. For example, privacy rules govern how schools handle student records and how websites treat young children\'s data. Many states have also passed laws about deepfakes and AI use.','Laws change quickly, so check current rules when it matters. A safe rule: never use AI to imitate a real person without their clear permission, and never create embarrassing fake images of anyone.'], key:'If it pretends to be a real person without permission, do not make it.',
     q:[Q('What is a deepfake?','An AI-made fake of a real person',['A fast computer','A privacy setting'],'Deepfakes imitate real people.'),Q('Is there one single US law covering all AI?','No, many laws apply',['Yes, one law covers everything','No laws apply'],'Rules are spread across many laws.'),Q('A friend asks you to make a fake video of a classmate as a joke. What should you do?','Refuse and explain why',['Do it quickly','Post it anonymously'],'It could harm the classmate and break rules or laws.')],
     g:{type:'scenario',title:'Deepfake decision',intro:'A situation comes up online. Choose carefully.',steps:[{q:'You see a video of a teacher saying something shocking. It looks real. First step?',opts:[['Check whether it is verified before sharing',3,'Verify before you spread anything.'],['Share it immediately',0,'Sharing unverified content can spread harm.'],['Argue in the comments',1,'Check facts first.']]},{q:'You find out it is a deepfake. What do you do?',opts:[['Report it to the platform and tell a trusted adult',3,'Reporting stops harm.'],['Keep it as a joke',0,'It is hurting a real person.'],['Ignore it',1,'Reporting helps.']]},{q:'A friend wants to make a fake voice message of a classmate. You…',opts:[['Say no and explain it could hurt them',3,'Impersonation without consent is harmful.'],['Say okay, just once',0,'Even one time can cause harm.'],['Stay silent',1,'Speaking up helps.']]}]}},
   { t:'Transparency and Limitations', v:[['Transparency','Being open about when and how AI is used'],['Disclose','Tell others that you used AI'],['Hallucination','When an AI states false information as if it were true'],['Limitation','A thing a tool cannot do reliably']],
     a:['Transparency means being open about when and how AI is used. When you disclose, you tell others that AI helped. This could be in a note on your assignment or a message to a teacher.','AI has limitations. One is hallucination, when an AI makes up facts, quotes or sources that sound real but are false.'],
     b:['Disclosure keeps trust. If your class allows AI, say how you used it, like "I used AI to brainstorm and then wrote the essay myself." If you are not sure it is allowed, ask first.','Hallucinations happen because AI predicts likely words, not verified facts. The more specific and unusual your question, the more careful you need to be.'], key:'Be open about AI use. Never trust a made-up-sounding source.',
     q:[Q('What is a hallucination?','AI stating false information as true',['A dream','A slow computer'],'Hallucinations are confident mistakes.'),Q('What does disclose mean?','Tell others you used AI',['Hide it','Delete it'],'Disclosure is openness.'),Q('You are unsure if AI use is allowed. You should…','Ask your teacher first',['Use it secretly','Assume yes'],'Ask before using.')],
     g:{type:'sort',title:'Honest or hidden?',intro:'Sort each action.',prompt:'Is the student being transparent?',buckets:['Transparent','Not transparent'],items:[['Adds a note: I used AI to brainstorm, then wrote this myself',0],['Submits AI-written work as entirely their own',1],['Asks the teacher if AI help is allowed',0],['Hides the AI chat history',1],['Cites which tool helped with an outline',0]],why:'Honest use is open use.'}},
   { t:'Fact-Checking AI', v:[['Fact-check','Verify a claim using trusted sources'],['Source','Where information comes from'],['Cross-check','Compare a claim across several independent sources'],['Credible','Believable and trustworthy']],
     a:['To fact-check, find out whether a claim is true by looking at trusted sources. A credible source is written by experts, shows its evidence and has a good reputation.','Cross-checking means comparing a claim across several independent sources. If only the AI says it, be suspicious.'],
     b:['AI can be wrong in ways that sound convincing. For example, it might say a famous event happened in the wrong year, or invent a book that does not exist.','A good routine: find the key claim, search for it in two reliable sources, and look for names, dates and numbers that match. When sources disagree, dig deeper.'], key:'Two credible sources beat one confident chatbot.',
     q:[Q('What does cross-checking mean?','Compare a claim across independent sources',['Ask the AI again','Trust the first answer'],'Independent agreement builds confidence.'),Q('Which source is most credible?','A government or university website with cited evidence',['An anonymous post','A random comment'],'Experts and evidence matter.'),Q('An AI gives a book title you cannot find anywhere. What probably happened?','It made up the title',['The book is secret','The internet is broken'],'Hallucinated sources are common.')],
     g:{type:'spot',title:'Catch the AI mistakes',intro:'An AI wrote a short summary. Some claims are wrong.',prompt:'Tap each claim that is false or unreliable.',lines:[['Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius at sea level.',false],['The Great Wall of China is visible from the Moon with the naked eye.',true,'This is a popular myth. It is not visible to the naked eye from the Moon.'],['Humans have five senses, and sight is one of them.',false],['According to the 2019 book Quantum Frogs by Dr. Lin, frogs can teleport.',true,'This looks like a made-up source and an absurd claim.'],['The Earth orbits the Sun.',false]]}},
   { t:'Privacy: What Not to Share', v:[['Personal information','Details that identify you, like your full name, address or school'],['Sensitive data','Information that could cause harm if exposed'],['Data retention','How long a company keeps your information'],['Phishing','A trick to get you to reveal private information']],
     a:['Personal information is anything that can identify you: full name, address, phone number, school, photos and passwords. Sensitive data, like health details, can cause harm if exposed.','Many AI tools store what you type. Data retention is how long they keep it. Scammers also use phishing messages to trick you into handing over details.'],
     b:['A simple rule: if you would not post it on a billboard, do not type it into a chatbot. Never share passwords, home address, ID numbers, private photos or other people\'s secrets.','You can still get help without sharing details. Replace names with "my friend" and use general descriptions instead of your real information.'], key:'Do not type anything into AI that you would not want public.',
     q:[Q('Which is safest to share with a chatbot?','A general question about how volcanoes work',['Your home address','Your password'],'General topics reveal nothing private.'),Q('What is phishing?','A trick to get your private information',['A fishing hobby','A password tool'],'Phishing steals info.'),Q('How can you get help without sharing details?','Use general descriptions instead of names',['Share everything','Use your password'],'Generalize to protect privacy.')],
     g:{type:'sort',title:'Safe or unsafe to type?',intro:'Sort the examples.',prompt:'Should you type this into an AI chatbot?',buckets:['Safe','Unsafe'],items:[['How do plants make food?',0],['My full name, address and phone number',1],['Explain fractions with a pizza example',0],['My school login password',1],['A photo of my friend\'s private message',1],['Give me five ideas for a science fair project',0]],why:'Keep private information private.'}},
   { t:'Attachments and Uploads', v:[['Attachment','A file you upload to a tool, like a photo or document'],['Metadata','Hidden details inside a file, like time and location'],['Malware','Harmful software'],['Permission','Approval to use something that belongs to someone else']],
     a:['Many AI tools let you upload files. An attachment can hold much more than you notice. Photos can contain metadata, hidden details like where and when they were taken.','Unknown files may carry malware, harmful software. Always check you have permission before uploading something that belongs to someone else.'],
     b:['Before uploading, ask: Does this file contain private details about me or others? Do I have permission? Is this tool trusted by my school?','Uploading a class roster, a classmate\'s photo or a private document can break privacy rules. When in doubt, remove personal details first or do not upload.'], key:'Uploading is sharing. Check the file and ask permission first.',
     q:[Q('Which file is the riskiest to upload?','A photo of a classmate without asking',['A blank practice worksheet','A made-up story you wrote'],'Photos of others need permission.'),Q('What is metadata?','Hidden details inside a file',['The file\'s color','A kind of virus'],'It can include location and time.'),Q('Before uploading someone else\'s work you should…','Get their permission',['Hope it is fine','Rename it'],'Permission matters.')],
     g:{type:'spot',title:'Upload safety check',intro:'A student plans to upload these files. Spot the unsafe ones.',prompt:'Tap each upload that is unsafe.',lines:[['A paragraph I wrote about my summer, with names removed',false],['A screenshot of a group chat with classmates\' full names',true,'It includes other people\'s private details.'],['A photo of my ID card to help me write a caption',true,'Never upload ID documents.'],['A blank worksheet for practice questions',false],['A spreadsheet of my school\'s student contact list',true,'This contains private information about many people.']]}},
   { t:'Bias and Fairness', v:[['Bias','An unfair leaning toward or against something'],['Stereotype','An oversimplified belief about a group'],['Representation','Whether different groups appear fairly in data'],['Audit','A careful check to find problems']],
     a:['Bias is an unfair leaning for or against something. AI learns from data made by people, so it can pick up human biases. A stereotype is an oversimplified belief about a group.','Representation matters. If training data has too few examples of some groups, the AI may perform worse for them. An audit is a careful check for problems like this.'],
     b:['Bias can show up in image tools that show only one type of person for a job, or in a system that mislabels some voices more often.','You can reduce harm by noticing patterns, asking for varied examples, and not treating AI output as neutral truth. Report problems when you see them.'], key:'AI can copy human bias. Stay alert and do not treat it as neutral.',
     q:[Q('Why can AI be biased?','It learns from human-made data',['It has opinions','It is always neutral'],'Data reflects people.'),Q('What is a stereotype?','An oversimplified belief about a group',['A proven fact','A type of algorithm'],'Stereotypes ignore individuals.'),Q('What is an audit?','A careful check for problems',['A kind of game','A type of prompt'],'Audits find issues.')],
     g:{type:'scenario',title:'Fix the biased tool',intro:'You notice an image AI shows only men when asked for "a scientist".',steps:[{q:'What is the best first move?',opts:[['Ask for varied examples and note the pattern',3,'Specific prompts and noticing the pattern are good first steps.'],['Say AI is always neutral',0,'AI reflects its data.'],['Stop thinking about it',1,'Awareness matters.']]},{q:'You want to report the issue. You should…',opts:[['Use the tool\'s feedback option with clear details',3,'Feedback helps improve tools.'],['Post an angry rant',1,'Clear reports are more useful.'],['Do nothing',0,'Reports help.']]},{q:'Using the tool for a class poster, what do you do?',opts:[['Adjust the prompt and choose fair images',3,'You can guide the output.'],['Use the biased image anyway',0,'You are responsible for the final work.'],['Skip the poster',1,'Better to fix and continue.']]}]}},
   { t:'Academic Integrity with AI', v:[['Academic integrity','Being honest in your schoolwork'],['Plagiarism','Using someone else\'s work as your own'],['Citation','A note showing where information or help came from'],['Policy','The official rules of a school or class']],
     a:['Academic integrity means being honest in schoolwork. Plagiarism is using someone else\'s work as your own, and that includes copying text from AI without saying so.','A citation shows where information or help came from. Every class has a policy, the official rules about what tools you may use.'],
     b:['Different teachers allow different uses. Some allow AI for brainstorming but not for writing. Some allow none. The policy in your class is the rule, even if another class is different.','If AI is allowed for help, use it to learn. Ask it to quiz you, explain a step, or check your reasoning. The best use is the one that leaves you smarter.'], key:'Follow the class policy and use AI to learn, not to skip learning.',
     q:[Q('What is plagiarism?','Using someone else\'s work as your own',['Writing a draft','Citing sources'],'AI-written text submitted as yours counts too.'),Q('Whose rules decide if you can use AI on an assignment?','The class or school policy',['Your friend\'s opinion','The AI tool\'s ads'],'Follow the policy.'),Q('Which is the best use of AI for studying?','Ask it to quiz you',['Have it write your essay','Copy its answers on a test'],'Learning use is best.')],
     g:{type:'sort',title:'Integrity check',intro:'Sort each action.',prompt:'Is this honest use of AI in a class that allows brainstorming only?',buckets:['Allowed','Not allowed'],items:[['Ask AI for three topic ideas, then pick your own',0],['Have AI write the entire essay',1],['Ask AI to quiz you on vocabulary',0],['Paste AI text and change a few words',1],['Ask AI to explain a concept you did not understand',0]],why:'Brainstorming and studying help you learn. Submitting AI text as yours does not.'}},
   { t:'AI at School and at Work', v:[['Workplace policy','Rules for using tools at a job'],['Confidential','Meant to be kept secret'],['Disclosure','Telling others when AI was used'],['Professional','Showing responsibility and care at work']],
     a:['Workplaces have rules about AI too. A workplace policy tells employees which tools are allowed. Information that is confidential, such as customer details, should never go into a public AI tool.','Professional use means being honest, protecting private information and reviewing every result before it goes out.'],
     b:['Companies care about AI skills, but they care even more about trust. An employee who leaks private data or sends unchecked AI output can cause real damage.','Practice now. The habits you build in class, like disclosing use, checking facts and protecting privacy, are the habits employers want.'], key:'Good AI habits at school become professional skills at work.',
     q:[Q('What should never go into a public AI tool at work?','Confidential customer information',['A general idea for a poster','A public holiday date'],'Confidential info stays private.'),Q('Who sets rules for AI at a job?','The workplace policy',['The AI','Your friends'],'Workplaces have policies.'),Q('Before sending AI-written work to a client you should…','Review and verify it',['Send it right away','Hide that it was AI'],'Review everything.')],
     g:{type:'scenario',title:'First day at the job',intro:'You are a new intern using AI. Choose professionally.',steps:[{q:'Your manager asks for a quick customer email. What do you do?',opts:[['Check the policy, draft with AI without customer details, then review',3,'Policy first, privacy always, review before sending.'],['Paste the customer\'s private info into a chatbot',0,'That could expose confidential data.'],['Send the first AI draft unchecked',1,'Always review.']]},{q:'You find an error in the AI draft after sending. What now?',opts:[['Tell your manager and fix it',3,'Honesty and quick fixes build trust.'],['Hope nobody notices',0,'Hiding mistakes makes them worse.'],['Blame the AI in public',1,'You are responsible for what you send.']]},{q:'A coworker asks if you used AI. You say…',opts:[['Yes, and explain how you checked it',3,'Transparency builds trust.'],['No, to avoid questions',0,'Do not lie.'],['Change the subject',1,'Be honest.']]}]}},
   { t:'Chapter 2 Field Review', v:[['Consent','Clear permission given freely'],['Hallucination','AI stating false information as true'],['Transparency','Being open about AI use'],['Fact-check','Verify a claim with trusted sources']],
     a:['This chapter covered rights, laws, honesty, fact-checking, privacy, uploads, bias, integrity and work. Review the key terms before the final test.','Keep your own checklist: Is it allowed? Is it private? Is it true? Is it fair? Am I being honest?'],
     b:['Responsible use is not about fear. It is about making smart choices so AI helps without harming you or others.','In the next chapter you will use AI as a thinking partner to brainstorm and find ideas.'], key:'Allowed, private, true, fair, honest.',
     q:[Q('Which is a hallucination?','An AI inventing a source that does not exist',['A correct answer','A slow reply'],'Made-up sources are hallucinations.'),Q('What should you check before sharing a surprising AI claim?','Trusted sources',['How many words it has','Whether it sounds confident'],'Verify before sharing.'),Q('What should you always do with AI-made content you present as your work?','Follow policy and be honest about AI use',['Hide it','Claim it is all yours'],'Honesty is key.')],
     g:{type:'spot',title:'Audit the student',intro:'A student wrote about how they use AI. Find the irresponsible habits.',prompt:'Tap every irresponsible habit.',lines:[['I ask AI to quiz me before tests.',false],['I paste my full address into chatbots to get better advice.',true,'Never share personal information.'],['I copy AI essays and submit them as mine.',true,'That is plagiarism.'],['I check surprising facts in two trusted sources.',false],['I tell my teacher when AI helped me brainstorm.',false]]}}
  ]};

  const ch3 = { id:'b3', title:'AI for Brainstorming', lessons:[
   { t:'Brainstorming with AI', v:[['Brainstorm','Generate many ideas without judging them yet'],['Divergent thinking','Coming up with many different ideas'],['Convergent thinking','Narrowing ideas down to the best one'],['Collaborate','Work together with another person or tool']],
     a:['To brainstorm is to produce many ideas without judging them yet. That is divergent thinking. Later you use convergent thinking to narrow down to the best idea.','AI can collaborate with you, working as a partner that offers extra ideas and angles.'],
     b:['AI is good at quantity. It can give you twenty ideas in seconds. You are better at choosing. You know your interests, your audience and your assignment.','Start with your own ideas first, then ask AI for more. This keeps the project yours and avoids copying what everyone else gets from the same prompt.'], key:'You choose. AI offers options.',
     q:[Q('Which comes first in brainstorming?','Producing lots of ideas',['Picking the final one','Grading the ideas'],'Diverge, then converge.'),Q('What is convergent thinking?','Narrowing to the best idea',['Making many ideas','Avoiding ideas'],'Converge means come together.'),Q('Why start with your own ideas?','It keeps the project yours',['AI cannot help','It saves no time'],'Own ideas first.')],
     g:{type:'order',title:'The brainstorm path',intro:'Put the brainstorm in order.',prompt:'Click in order.',items:['Write your own quick ideas first','Ask AI for more angles','Collect everything in one list','Narrow to your top three','Choose the best fit for your assignment']}},
   { t:'Finding Sources', v:[['Primary source','Original evidence, like a letter, data set or interview'],['Secondary source','A source that explains or analyzes primary sources'],['Keyword','A word used to search'],['Database','An organized collection of information you can search']],
     a:['Primary sources are original evidence. Secondary sources explain or analyze them. Good research uses both.','Keywords are the words you search with. A database, like your school library\'s, is an organized collection of trusted material.'],
     b:['AI can suggest keywords and places to look. It should not be treated as the source itself. Always follow the idea back to a real article, book or website.','In the simulation you will enter your own idea and get search terms and trusted places to look. Then you will decide how to judge what you find.'], key:'Use AI to find where to look. The real source is the proof.',
     q:[Q('A diary from 1850 is a…','Primary source',['Secondary source','Keyword'],'Original evidence is primary.'),Q('What is the safest use of AI in research?','Getting search terms and places to look',['Using it as the final source','Citing it instead of real sources'],'Follow ideas back to real sources.'),Q('A keyword is…','A word you search with',['A password','A type of source'],'Keywords guide search.')],
     g:{type:'lab',kind:'sources',title:'Source finder lab',intro:'Enter your own research idea and see how a practice AI suggests ways to find sources.'}},
   { t:'Organizing Your Thoughts', v:[['Cluster','A group of related ideas'],['Outline','A plan showing the main points in order'],['Theme','A main idea that connects several points'],['Priority','What matters most or should be done first']],
     a:['When you have many ideas, group them. A cluster is a set of related ideas. A theme is the main idea that connects them.','An outline is a plan showing main points in order. Priority tells you what should come first.'],
     b:['AI can quickly sort a messy list into groups. You then check whether the groups make sense and decide the priority.','Sorting is a helper step, not a final one. AI may misplace an idea that only makes sense in your project, so always review its grouping.'], key:'AI sorts. You decide what matters.',
     q:[Q('What is a cluster?','A group of related ideas',['One long sentence','A type of file'],'Clusters group ideas.'),Q('What is an outline?','A plan with main points in order',['A drawing','A password'],'Outlines organize writing.'),Q('If AI groups an idea wrongly you should…','Move it yourself',['Leave it','Delete the project'],'You are in charge.')],
     g:{type:'lab',kind:'organize',title:'Idea dump organizer',intro:'Dump all your ideas and watch a practice AI sort them into themes.'}},
   { t:'Idea Dumping', v:[['Idea dump','Writing down every idea quickly without editing'],['Free writing','Writing without stopping to fix or judge'],['Prompt starter','A short line that gets ideas flowing'],['Filter','A way to remove ideas that do not fit']],
     a:['An idea dump means writing every idea quickly without editing. Free writing is writing without stopping to fix or judge.','A prompt starter is a short line that gets ideas flowing, like "What problems do I notice at school?"'],
     b:['Messy first lists are normal. The goal is quantity, not quality. Later you use a filter to remove ideas that do not fit your goal.','AI can add fresh angles after your dump, but your original ideas show your own thinking.'], key:'Dump first, filter later.',
     q:[Q('What is the goal of an idea dump?','Quantity, not perfection',['One perfect idea','Short answers only'],'Get everything out first.'),Q('Free writing means…','Writing without stopping to judge',['Writing for free money','Writing only facts'],'Do not stop to fix.'),Q('When do you filter ideas?','After you dump them',['Before writing anything','Never'],'Filter later.')],
     g:{type:'order',title:'Idea dump drill',intro:'Order a good dumping session.',prompt:'Click in order.',items:['Set a timer for five minutes','Write every idea without judging','Stop and read the whole list','Mark your favorites','Ask AI for angles you missed']}},
   { t:'Finding a Topic', v:[['Topic','The subject you will focus on'],['Broad','Too general to focus on'],['Narrow','Focused enough to research well'],['Interest','What you genuinely care about']],
     a:['Your topic is the subject you focus on. A broad topic like "animals" is too general. A narrow topic like "how city lights affect bird migration" is focused.','The best topics match your interest and are narrow enough to research.'],
     b:['Ask AI to suggest topics, then check them: Do I care? Is it specific? Can I find good sources?','Different prompts give different results. In the simulation you will try suggested prompts and compare the ideas they produce.'], key:'Interesting, narrow, researchable.',
     q:[Q('Which topic is narrowest?','How city lights affect bird migration',['Animals','Nature','Science'],'Specific topics are easier to research.'),Q('Which question helps you pick a topic?','Do I care about this?',['Is it the first one?','Is it the shortest?'],'Interest keeps you going.'),Q('A good topic is also…','Researchable',['Impossible to find sources for','Secret'],'You need sources.')],
     g:{type:'lab',kind:'topic',title:'Topic finder lab',intro:'Run a suggested prompt in the practice AI and see the ideas.',prompts:[['Give me 5 narrow topic ideas about technology in schools',['How AI tutors help students practice math','Do phone-free classrooms improve focus?','How schools can teach internet safety better','What makes a good school app','How students use study videos']],['Suggest 5 science project ideas I can do at home with a budget under $10',['Which paper towel absorbs the most water?','Does music change plant growth?','How does temperature affect battery life?','Which materials make the best insulator for a cup','Does light color change how fast plants grow?']],['Give me 5 ideas for a history paper about my local community',['How my town changed after the railroad arrived','The story behind a local landmark','Who were the first families in my area','A local business that lasted 100 years','How my school was founded']]]}},
   { t:'Better Brainstorm Prompts', v:[['Constraint','A limit that guides ideas, like budget or time'],['Audience','The people the work is for'],['Format','The shape of an answer, like a list or a table'],['Follow-up','A next question that builds on an answer']],
     a:['Strong prompts include constraints, such as budget or time; an audience, the people the work is for; and a format, like a list of five ideas.','A follow-up is a next question that builds on the answer. Good brainstorming is a conversation.'],
     b:['Compare "Give me ideas" with "Give me 5 ideas for a 3-minute class presentation about recycling, for 9th graders, as a bulleted list." The second gets more useful results.','If results feel generic, add details about yourself, your goal and your limits, but never private information.'], key:'Constraint, audience, format, follow-up.',
     q:[Q('Which prompt is strongest?','Give me 5 ideas for a 3-minute presentation about recycling for 9th graders',['Ideas please','Help'],'It includes constraints, audience and format.'),Q('What is an audience?','The people the work is for',['A type of AI','The prompt itself'],'Know who it is for.'),Q('A follow-up helps you…','Build on the answer',['End the chat','Delete the answer'],'Brainstorming is a conversation.')],
     g:{type:'sort',title:'Strong or weak prompt?',intro:'Sort the prompts.',prompt:'Is this a strong brainstorming prompt?',buckets:['Strong','Weak'],items:[['Ideas',1],['Give me 6 ideas for a one-minute video about healthy sleep for teens',0],['Do my project',1],['List 5 low-cost fundraiser ideas for a school club of 12 people',0],['Something cool',1]],why:'Strong prompts have a goal, limits and context.'}},
   { t:'Eliminating Bad Ideas', v:[['Criteria','Standards used to judge ideas'],['Feasible','Possible to do with your time and resources'],['Ethical','Fair, honest and safe for others'],['Eliminate','Remove from the list']],
     a:['Criteria are the standards you use to judge ideas. Common ones are feasible, meaning possible with your time and resources, and ethical, meaning fair, honest and safe.','To eliminate an idea is to remove it. Cutting weak ideas makes room for strong ones.'],
     b:['Some ideas fail because they are too big. Others fail because they break rules or hurt people, like copying someone else\'s work or sharing private information.','AI can help flag risky ideas, but it can also miss them. You always make the final decision.'], key:'Use criteria. Cut what is not feasible or ethical.',
     q:[Q('Which idea should be eliminated?','Copy a finished essay online and change a few words',['Interview three local experts','Survey classmates'],'Copying is dishonest.'),Q('What does feasible mean?','Possible with your time and resources',['Fun','Famous'],'Check what you can actually do.'),Q('Who makes the final call on cutting ideas?','You',['The AI alone','Nobody'],'You stay in charge.')],
     g:{type:'lab',kind:'eliminate',title:'Eliminate the bad idea',intro:'Enter four ideas, including one you know is bad. See if the practice AI catches it.',topic:'a class project'}},
   { t:'Voice Notes and Dictation', v:[['Dictation','Speaking so a tool types your words'],['Transcript','The written version of something spoken'],['Voice note','A recorded spoken message'],['Summary','A short version with the main points']],
     a:['Dictation means speaking so a tool types your words. The written result is a transcript. A voice note is a recorded spoken message.','Many people think faster by talking. Dictation helps you capture ideas before you lose them.'],
     b:['After dictating, read the transcript. Speech tools can mishear names and terms. A summary condenses the main points but may drop details you care about.','Privacy matters. A recording can include other people\'s voices or private details. Get permission and avoid recording sensitive conversations.'], key:'Talk to capture, read to correct, ask before recording others.',
     q:[Q('What is a transcript?','The written version of something spoken',['A recording','A summary'],'Transcripts are text.'),Q('Before recording a conversation with a friend you should…','Ask their permission',['Record secretly','Post it'],'Consent matters.'),Q('Why re-read a dictated transcript?','Tools can mishear words',['It is always perfect','It is boring'],'Always check.')],
     g:{type:'order',title:'Voice-note workflow',intro:'Put a good voice-note process in order.',prompt:'Click in order.',items:['Get permission if others will be recorded','Speak your ideas out loud','Read the transcript and fix mistakes','Ask AI for a short summary','Check the summary against your own notes']}},
   { t:'Combining and Refining Ideas', v:[['Combine','Merge two ideas into a stronger one'],['Refine','Improve something by adding detail'],['Prototype','An early rough version to test'],['Feedback','Comments that help you improve']],
     a:['Great ideas are often combinations. To combine is to merge two ideas into a stronger one. To refine is to improve it by adding detail.','A prototype is a rough early version you can test. Feedback is what other people tell you about it.'],
     b:['Ask AI to suggest combinations, then pick one and refine it. Make a quick prototype, such as an outline or a sketch, and ask classmates for feedback.','Remember to credit help. If AI or a classmate shaped your idea, say so when it is appropriate.'], key:'Combine, refine, test, improve.',
     q:[Q('What is a prototype?','A rough early version to test',['A finished product','A type of AI'],'Prototypes help you learn.'),Q('What does refine mean?','Improve by adding detail',['Delete','Ignore'],'Refine means improve.'),Q('Feedback is…','Comments that help you improve',['An insult','A grade only'],'Use it to get better.')],
     g:{type:'match',title:'Match the step',intro:'Match each step to its meaning.',pairs:[['Combine','Merge two ideas into a stronger one'],['Refine','Add detail and improve'],['Prototype','A rough version to test'],['Feedback','Comments that help you improve']]}},
   { t:'Chapter 3 Field Review', v:[['Divergent thinking','Coming up with many ideas'],['Convergent thinking','Narrowing to the best'],['Criteria','Standards used to judge ideas'],['Prototype','An early version to test']],
     a:['You have practiced brainstorming, finding sources, organizing, dumping ideas, choosing topics, prompting, eliminating weak ideas and using voice notes.','Review the key terms before the final test.'],
     b:['The pattern is: generate, organize, narrow, test. AI helps with each step, but you decide. Your ideas, your judgment, your name on the work.','In the next chapter you will turn ideas into plans using calendars, graphs, notes and tasks.'], key:'Generate, organize, narrow, test.',
     q:[Q('Narrowing to the best idea is…','Convergent thinking',['Divergent thinking','Free writing'],'Converge means narrow.'),Q('Who makes the final decisions about your ideas?','You',['The AI','A random choice'],'You decide.'),Q('Why use criteria?','To judge ideas fairly',['To create more ideas','To avoid choosing'],'Criteria make decisions clearer.')],
     g:{type:'scenario',title:'Run a brainstorm',intro:'Your club needs a fundraiser idea. Make good choices.',steps:[{q:'You start by…',opts:[['Dumping your own ideas, then asking AI for more',3,'Own ideas first, then AI angles.'],['Asking AI to choose for you',1,'You should choose.'],['Copying another club\'s plan',0,'Copying is not creative or fair.']]},{q:'Two ideas look good. What do you do?',opts:[['Compare them using criteria like cost, time and fairness',3,'Criteria make the choice clear.'],['Flip a coin',1,'Criteria are better.'],['Pick the one AI said first',0,'You decide, not the first answer.']]},{q:'Your friend gave a great suggestion. You…',opts:[['Credit them and thank them',3,'Credit others.'],['Say it was all you',0,'Be honest.'],['Hide it',1,'Give credit.']]}]}}
  ]};

  window.COURSE = { pathways:[
    { id:'beginner', name:'Rookie Agent', sub:'Beginner pathway. Learn what AI is, how to use it responsibly, and how to think with it.', who:'Start here if AI is new to you or you want a strong foundation.', chapters:[ch1,ch2,ch3] },
    { id:'intermediate', name:'Field Agent', sub:'Intermediate pathway. Build real skills in content, data, prompting and apps.', who:'For students who know the basics and want to do more.', chapters:[] },
    { id:'advanced', name:'Director', sub:'Advanced pathway. Ethics, the workforce and leading with AI.', who:'For students ready to lead and make big decisions.', chapters:[] }
  ]};
})();
